:["Move the clue = revision (meaning). Spelling and full stop = edits.","Careful use of publish with clear evidence.","Publishing a neat copy of a confusing plot still leaves readers lost.","Revising improves meaning and clarity for an audience before publishing; editing surface errors comes after the ideas work."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising revise?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named publish, not by copying the model.","options":["Apply the idea with new evidence and name the publish used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a missing detail as a stated fact."],"question":"A learner is practising revise. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries revise.","options":["Publishing a neat copy of a confusing plot still leaves readers lost.","Point to exact words in the sentence that show revise and say what they do.","Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event.","Say the writing is good without naming any words."],"question":"Read: \"In the dusty shed, Lila oiled the bike chain before the race.\" Which choice best uses revise from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill; the others miss the evidence.","options":["Copy the model word-for-word and call that independent work.","Move the clue = revision (meaning). Spelling and full stop = edits.","Publishing a neat copy of a confusing plot still leaves readers lost.","Ignore the words and invent a more exciting event."],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about revise?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect.","options":["Locate a specific word, phrase or structural clue before deciding.","Guess from the title alone with no textual clue.","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged.","Treat a missing detail as a stated fact."],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to practise publish?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding.","options":["Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length.","Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea.","Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake.","Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess."],"question":"Which revision actually repairs meaning?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice.","options":["Retype the worked model as the final answer.","Change fonts and claim the work is new.","Publishing a neat copy of a confusing plot still leaves readers lost.","Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about revise."],"question":"Which response shows transfer of revise to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating revise on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Move the clue = revision (meaning). Spelling and full stop = edits. Reasoning: I used revise by attending to publish rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with revise, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Publishing a neat copy of a confusing plot still leaves readers lost. Correction: Move the clue = revision (meaning). Spelling and full stop = edits. Why: the correction restores accurate use of revise.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-eng-y4-w05-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise revise.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail helps a reader understand.","A vague compliment is weaker than quoted evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with a stronger revision.","Change meaning or accuracy, not only length.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh topic that still uses the same skill.","Explain one decision in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include an original response and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new content."]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use revise with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify publish and explain why it matters.","I can practise meaning independently on a new prompt.","I can notice audience needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Revising improves meaning and clarity for an audience before publishing; editing surface errors comes after the ideas work. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task.","options":["A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation.","Revising improves meaning and clarity for an audience before publishing; editing surface errors comes after the ideas work."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about revise?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nRevision: add the latch clue earlier so the ending feels fair. Edit later: capitalise Dad, fix \"runing\" → \"running\". Publish with a title that hints at the problem.\n\nTRY FIRST\nSort: fix spelling of because; move the clue to paragraph 2; add a full stop. Which is revision vs edit?\n\nFEEDBACK\nMove the clue = revision (meaning). Spelling and full stop = edits.","explain":"Revising improves meaning and clarity for an audience before publishing; editing surface errors comes after the ideas work.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Publishing a neat copy of a confusing plot still leaves readers lost. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Meaning revision before surface edits.","caption":"Audience clarity comes first.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Revise</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">move clue earlier</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Edit</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">capitals + spelling</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Revision: add the latch clue earlier so the ending feels fair. Edit later: capitalise Dad, fix \"runing\" → \"running\". Pub Then write your own attempt.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Revision: add the latch clue earlier so the ending feels fair. Edit later: capitalise Dad, fix \"runing\" → \"running\". Pub Then write your own attempt."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader. A supported explanation follows the actual words and purpose of the task.","options":["Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no explanation."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about revise?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nRevision: add the latch clue earlier so the ending feels fair. Edit later: capitalise Dad, fix \"runing\" → \"running\". Publish with a title that hints at the problem. Think about how this word or relationship creates meaning.\n\nTRY FIRST\nUnderline or quote one precise detail in the model and explain its effect.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning.","explain":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Sayi